---
import { calculateProjectRate, calculateRetainer, convertPay, priceFromMargin } from "@MathMyRate/calculators";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import DataTable from "../../components/DataTable.astro";
import { hubBreadcrumbs, toolsInHub } from "../../data/tools";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { formatUsdGrouped } from "../../scripts/calculator-form";

const breadcrumbs = hubBreadcrumbs("freelance");

const freelanceTools = toolsInHub("freelance");

const summaryRows = [
  ["Freelance Hourly Rate Calculator", "Take-home target, costs, and working year", "Hourly and day rate"],
  ["Freelance Project Rate Calculator", "Hourly rate, hours, contingency, and expenses", "Fixed project quote"],
  ["Freelance Retainer Calculator", "Hourly rate, included hours, and discount", "Monthly fee and effective rate"],
  ["Markup and Margin Calculator", "Cost, and a markup, margin, or price", "Price, profit, markup, and margin"],
  ["Salary to Hourly Calculator", "Pay for any period, hours, and weeks", "Pay per hour, day, week, month, and year"],
  ["Early Payment Discount Calculator", "Invoice amount and terms such as 2/10 net 30", "Discount, early payment, and its yearly cost"],
  ["Rate Increase Calculator", "Current rate, the raise, and billable hours", "New rate, monthly and yearly change, hours you could lose"],
  ["1099 vs W-2 Rate Calculator", "Salary, benefits, expenses, and billable hours", "Contractor income and hourly rate that match the salary"],
];

const quote = calculateProjectRate({ hourlyRateCents: 9_000n, estimatedMinutes: 1_800, directExpensesCents: 60_000n, contingencyBps: 1_000 });

const retainer = calculateRetainer({ hourlyRateCents: 9_000n, includedMinutes: 600, discountBps: 500, usedMinutes: 600, months: 6 });

const resale = priceFromMargin({ costCents: 60_000n, marginBps: 2_000 });

const offer = convertPay({ amountCents: 9_000n, period: "hour", hoursPerWeekHundredths: 4_000, daysPerWeek: 5, weeksPerYear: 52 });
---
<Layout title="Freelance Calculators: Rates & Retainers | MathMyRate" description="Free freelance pricing calculators: hourly and day rates, project quotes, monthly retainers, markup and margin, and salary to hourly pay, all exact to the cent." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-10 md:px-8 md:py-16">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />
    <h1 class="display mt-4 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-bold leading-tight md:text-5xl">Freelance Pricing Calculators</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Start with the work. Then price it properly.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/70">Find a rate that covers your year, then turn it into project quotes and retainers, price resold costs, set payment terms, plan a raise, and compare against a salary.</p>
    <ul class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {freelanceTools.map((tool) => (
        <li>
          <a href={tool.href} class="block h-full rounded-[1.5rem] border border-line bg-[#fafbfd] p-7 transition-transform hover:-translate-y-1">
            <h2 class="display text-2xl font-bold">{tool.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-3 text-ink/65">{tool.summary}</p>
            <span class="mt-6 block font-semibold text-navy">Open →</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <article class="guide mt-16 max-w-3xl border-t border-line pt-4">
      <h2>Every freelance calculator at a glance</h2>
      <DataTable caption="Freelance calculators, what they need, and what they give" headers={["Calculator", "You enter", "You get"]} rows={summaryRows} />

      <h2>Who these calculators are for</h2>
      <p>They are for independent workers who set their own prices: designers, developers, writers, consultants, and anyone else who quotes clients directly. They are also useful before you go independent, when you need to know what freelancing has to pay to replace a job.</p>

      <h2>How the calculators fit together</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Find your rate.</strong> The <a href="/freelance/hourly-rate-calculator/">hourly rate calculator</a> starts from the income you want and your realistic working year, and gives the hourly and day rate that supports it. Every other calculator builds on that number.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote projects.</strong> The <a href="/freelance/project-rate-calculator/">project rate calculator</a> turns the rate into a fixed price. At $90 an hour, a 30-hour project with 10% contingency and $600 of expenses is a {formatUsdGrouped(quote.targetReceiptsCents)} quote.</li>
        <li><strong>Sell ongoing work.</strong> The <a href="/freelance/retainer-calculator/">retainer calculator</a> prices a monthly commitment. Ten hours a month at the same rate, with a 5% discount, is {formatUsdGrouped(retainer.monthlyFeeCents)} a month.</li>
        <li><strong>Price resold costs.</strong> The <a href="/freelance/markup-margin-calculator/">markup and margin calculator</a> prices subcontracted work and products. Keeping a 20% margin on a $600 subcontract means charging {formatUsdGrouped(resale.priceCents)}.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare with a job.</strong> The <a href="/freelance/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">salary to hourly calculator</a> converts pay between periods. $90 an hour for a full-time year would be {formatUsdGrouped(offer.yearlyCents)}, though a freelancer bills far fewer hours than that. The <a href="/freelance/1099-vs-w2-calculator/">1099 vs W-2 calculator</a> goes further and works out the contractor income that matches a salary after Social Security and Medicare tax.</li>
        <li><strong>Get paid sooner, or charge more.</strong> The <a href="/freelance/early-payment-discount-calculator/">early payment discount calculator</a> prices terms like 2/10 net 30 and shows what that discount costs you as a yearly rate, and the <a href="/freelance/rate-increase-calculator/">rate increase calculator</a> shows what a raise adds and how much work you could lose and still earn the same.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Then account for payment fees</h2>
      <p>If clients pay by card, PayPal, or a platform, part of every invoice goes to the provider. The <a href="/fees/">payment fee calculators</a> show what reaches you, and can work backward from a quote to the amount to invoice.</p>

      <h2>Exact to the cent</h2>
      <p>Every calculation uses whole cents, not rounded decimals. Where a result has to be rounded, it is rounded in the direction that protects you: a quote or a price is rounded up, so you never charge a fraction of a cent less than you meant to. The details are on <a href="/methodology/">how the numbers are made</a>.</p>

      <h2>Mistakes these calculators help you avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pricing every working hour as billable.</strong> Finding clients, admin, and gaps between projects fill part of every freelance week. The hourly rate calculator asks what share of your time you bill, so the rate covers the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>Treating a markup as a margin.</strong> Adding 25% to a subcontractor's invoice keeps 20% of the price, not 25%. The markup and margin calculator shows both numbers side by side.</li>
        <li><strong>Discounting a retainer below your floor.</strong> A retainer discount lowers every hour you deliver. The retainer calculator shows the rate per included hour, so you can check it against your hourly rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Comparing a freelance rate with a salary's hourly figure.</strong> A salary pays for every working hour and paid leave; a freelance rate has to cover unbilled time and costs as well. The salary to hourly calculator gives the starting number, and the hourly rate calculator gives the rate that replaces it.</li>
        <li><strong>Forgetting the payment fee.</strong> A client paying by card or PayPal sends less than the invoice total. The fee calculators work out what to invoice so you receive the full quote.</li>
        <li><strong>Offering an early payment discount without pricing it.</strong> Two percent off for paying twenty days sooner is worth more than 37% a year to the client. The early payment discount calculator turns any terms into that yearly rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Quoting a contract rate from a salary.</strong> A contractor pays both halves of Social Security and Medicare and buys their own benefits, so matching a salary takes more than the salary. The 1099 vs W-2 calculator works out how much more.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the calculators do not do</h2>
      <p>They do not calculate income tax, self-employment contributions, or benefits, and they do not know where you live. The hourly rate calculator uses a tax reserve you choose as a planning assumption. They also cannot tell you what clients in your field will pay. Use them to find the price that works for you, then set your rates with your market in mind.</p>

      <h2>Guides</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/guides/salary-vs-freelance-rate/">How to compare a salary with a freelance rate</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/markup-vs-margin/">Markup vs margin: what's the difference?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/how-to-cover-payment-processing-fees/">How to charge enough to cover payment processing fees</a></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/60">Planning estimates only; not tax, accounting, or legal advice.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
